What is ejs-mate?

👉 It adds layout support to EJS

So instead of repeating navbar/footer in every file, you do:

one layout file
pages get injected inside it
🔥 Why it’s better than <%- include %>
❌ includes method:
you manually add navbar in every file
easy to forget
repetitive
✅ ejs-mate:
one layout (like React template)
cleaner structure
professional approach
🚀 Setup ejs-mate
🔹 Step 1: Install
npm i ejs-mate
🔹 Step 2: Configure in app.js
const engine = require("ejs-mate");

app.engine("ejs", engine);
app.set("view engine", "ejs");
🧱 Step 3: Create layout file

📁 views/layouts/boilerplate.ejs

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Airbnb Clone</title>
</head>
<body>

    <nav>
        <a href="/listings">Home</a>
        <a href="/listings/new">Add Listing</a>
    </nav>

    <%- body %>

</body>
</html>
📄 Step 4: Use layout in pages
Example: show.ejs
<% layout('layouts/boilerplate') %>

<h3>Listing Details</h3>
🧠 How it works
layout file (boilerplate)
        ↓
navbar + structure
        ↓
<%- body %> replaced with page content
⚖️ ejs-mate vs include
Feature	include	ejs-mate
Setup	easy	medium
Clean code	medium	⭐ best
Scalable	no	yes
Real projects	less used	widely used
⚡ Final recommendation for YOU

Since you're building Airbnb clone:

👉 YES use ejs-mate
because:

you have multiple pages
layout reuse matters
cleaner architecture
🧠 One-line answer

👉 Yes, ejs-mate is better than manual includes for your project.